---
import ToolPageLayout from "../../layouts/ToolPageLayout.astro";
import EstimateBuilderIsland from "../../components/calculator/EstimateBuilderIsland.tsx";

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "What's the difference between a quote and an estimate?",
    answer:
      "Use the estimate builder to explain provisional quantities and selling rates. This quote builder adds a reference, a printed validity date, and a summary view for presenting service amounts without unit-price columns. Clearly state the scope and terms in either document; the tool does not decide the legal effect of your wording.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I add a validity period or deposit terms?",
    answer: "Use the Valid until field for the date printed on your quote. Put payment or deposit requirements in Notes. The builder does not collect deposits, send reminders, or enforce expiry.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I make sure my quote is actually profitable?",
    answer:
      "Run your numbers through the landscaping cost calculator first so your quote price reflects your true cost and target margin, not just a guess.",
  },
];
---

<ToolPageLayout
  title="Landscaping Quote Template — Free Quote Builder"
  description="Create a landscaping quote with a reference, validity date, service pricing, and detailed or summary lines. Print or save the customer document as a PDF."
  eyebrow="Free interactive template"
  h1="Landscaping Quote Template"
  intro="A customer-facing quote (also called a landscape quote template) you can send before the job is booked — your business, the customer, the services, and a total price. Fill it in, print it, or save it as a PDF."
  breadcrumbLabel="Landscaping Quote Template"
  currentHref="/landscaping-quote-template/"
  faqs={faqs}
  webApplicationSchema={false}
>
  <EstimateBuilderIsland variant="quote" client:load />

  <Fragment slot="extra">
    <article class="tool-editorial mb-10">
      <h2>Choose how much pricing detail to show</h2>
      <p>Detailed mode displays service quantities, units, unit prices, and line totals. Summary mode shows each service and its amount without the unit breakdown. Switching views changes presentation only; it never changes the quote total. Choose the format that explains the agreed scope most clearly to your customer.</p>
      <h2>Write the scope before setting the total</h2>
      <p>A useful quote explains what will be supplied, what preparation is included, and what cleanup or removal the price covers. For planting work, specify the agreed plant sizes and quantities. For mulch or soil work, record the measured area and intended additional depth. Do not rely on a short label such as “landscaping” to describe several different services.</p>
      <p>Give the quote an optional reference so you can identify it later. Set a Valid until date on or after the quote date; both fields appear in the printed document. Use Notes for scheduling assumptions, payment terms, and how scope changes will be handled. These fields do not collect a deposit, take signatures, send reminders, or enforce expiry. Confirm the final wording before sharing.</p>
      <h2>Example: a two-service customer quote</h2>
      <p>The sample mulch line totals $760 and the shrub line totals $1,170, for a $1,930 quote. In summary mode the customer sees the two service amounts and total. In detailed mode the same price includes eight yards at $95 and 18 plants at $65. The numbers are examples; check your own costs before using any rate.</p>
      <p>Test the final price in the <a href="/landscape-profit-margin-calculator/">profit margin calculator</a>. If the scope is still provisional, use the <a href="/landscaping-estimate-template/">estimate template</a> to document assumptions. After the job, create an <a href="/landscaping-invoice-template/">itemized invoice</a> and review the <a href="/landscape-job-cost-calculator/">actual job profit</a>. Free-page entries do not carry between tools.</p>
    </article>
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-border bg-paper p-6">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink">Know your price is right before you send it</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted">
        Use the <a href="/landscaping-cost-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-forest underline">landscaping cost calculator</a>
        to check your true cost and required margin before you commit to a quoted number.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</ToolPageLayout>
